mpc-uac2 add-on (built 2026-10-19, Todoboy task 2541 + 2548 + 2550) on Akai MPC 3.9.1 Gen1.
In STANDALONE mode the USB-B port carries audio both ways ~30 s after the MPC app starts, as
"MPC One+ Audio" / "MPC One Audio" (USB audio class 2, 2 ch, 44.1 kHz), plus one USB-MIDI port
("MPC One+ Port 1" / "MPC One Port 1"):
  computer -> MPC: audio the computer plays to the device is mixed into the MPC's inputs 1/2,
                   alongside the physical line inputs (saturating add; both keep working);
  MPC -> computer: the MPC's main stereo out is recorded by the computer;
  MIDI both ways:  the MPC app's ALSA sequencer client ("MPC") is auto-connected to the gadget's
                   f_midi port, so notes/clock flow to and from the computer (on by default; set
                   MIDI_PORTS=0 when starting the gadget for audio only).
The MPC's own outputs keep working. Controller mode is untouched (the add-on steps aside).
Each audio direction corrects the USB/MPC clock difference by slipping one frame now and then.
Active on MPC One+ and MPC One only (this Akai image also installs on X/Live/Key models, where the add-on stays dormant).
Only tested on an MPC One+; the MPC One USB ID comes from PolyVector (TheKikGen/MPC-LiveXplore issue 55).
Turn off: put an empty file named MPC-UAC2-OFF in the root of a USB stick or SD card and insert it.
If the MPC app ever crash-loops, the add-on switches itself off until the next reboot.
Remove completely: install the official Akai update image again.
Files: /usr/libexec/mpc-uac2/*, /usr/lib/systemd/system/mpc-uac2d.service (+ multi-user.target.wants link),
       /usr/lib/systemd/system/acvs.service.d/50-mpc-uac2-tee.conf. Logs: /tmp/mpc-uac2d.log, /tmp/mpc-uac2-tee.log

USB file transfer (add-on 1.3): MPCTransfer mounts automatically on the computer.
Drop files/folders, then Eject in Finder. The MPC copies and verifies them into
SD card / USB Imports / Transfer-<date>-<id>. Reconnect USB for another batch.
Wait for the new folder before removing the SD or entering Controller mode.
The private exFAT image uses 1 GiB in /data/mpc-uac2/transfer; the SD is never
shared as a block device. Missing/full SD: batch retained and retried. Unplug
without eject: reconnect, then eject. Existing files are never overwritten.
New service: mpc-transferd.service; logs: journalctl -u mpc-transferd.
